{
  "title": "About TimeBank Plugin",
  "story": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "21341e728c47230c",
      "text": "The TimeBank plugin displays a list of time entries as a dated ledger. Each line in the item text is one entry. Lines ending with a time amount are summed into a running total. The column header shows the end date of the batch. An entry can name a counterparty ledger \u2014 `Gardening for [[Alice Ledger]]: 2 hours` on the same site, or `Gardening for [https://alice.wiki/view/alices-ledger Alice's Ledger]: 2 hours` on another site \u2014 and a **Verified** badge checks that the other ledger records the same hours the other way round. Clicking the badge opens the **Ledger Verification Tool** page beside the ledger. Since 0.4.0 an entry's label can link the **Time Transaction** page that records the work, and a ledger with a `LINEUP` line pulls its entries from those pages. Labels and captions go through the wiki's link resolver, so [[wikilinks]] work everywhere in the item. See [[Table Formatting]] for the HTML markup used to render the table."
    },
    {
      "type": "timebank",
      "id": "c305fdd22a5ac038",
      "text": "start: 1 June 2026\nend: 7 June 2026\nMeeting with David: 2 hours\nCode review: 1 hour\nAdmin tasks\nCall with team: 30 mins"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "8952b36884fad15a",
      "text": "# Entering Time"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "e6bd0f633b537d7c",
      "text": "Double-click the item to edit entries. Each line is one entry. End a line with a time amount to include it in the total. Lines without a time amount are recorded as notes.\n\nSupported time formats:\n- `2 hours` or `1 hour`\n- `30 mins` or `45 minutes`\n- `1.5h` or `90m`\n- `2 hrs`"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "570314c34d9c6085",
      "text": "# Date Handling"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "7fcf2dca250dd60f",
      "text": "Add `start:` and `end:` lines to set the period. The end date appears as the column header in short format e.g. `7 June 2026`. The caption shows the full range e.g. `Monday, 1 June 2026 \u2013 Sunday, 7 June 2026`.\n\nAccepted date formats: `7 June 2026`, `June 7 2026`, `2026-06-07`\n\nDates are stored as `item.start` and `item.end` in milliseconds since the Unix epoch. If no `end:` line is present, the end date defaults to the time of the last edit."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "5324b88a2f3b51c2",
      "text": "# Commands\nLines that set the ledger up rather than record time"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "id": "c1cecc47bb6f159a",
      "text": "CAPTION Commands are never shown as entries or caption\n\n| Command | Example | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| START: | START: 1 September 2026 | Start of the period |\n| END: | END: 7 September 2026 | End of the period, shown as the column header |\n| NOTIFY: | NOTIFY: ntfy.sh/timebank-david | The ntfy topic the tool page's Send verification message button posts to; https unless you write http:// |\n| LINEUP | LINEUP | Pull entries from Time Transaction pages to the left in the lineup and on this site that name this ledger, marked as pulled (thaw) |\n| WATCH: | WATCH: ledger.timebank.private.fish | Sites this ledger watches for incoming Time Transaction pages that name it \u2014 a thank-you written on the receiver's site; their hours show as awaiting reconcile |\n| TOOL | TOOL | Makes the item the Ledger Verification Tool's report: it checks the ledger on the page to its left |\n| REVIEW: | REVIEW: 2026-W40 | On a weekly report: the week's frozen Review rows and an owner-only Approve button that writes APPROVED: date by name once (0.7.0) |\n| BOARD | BOARD | The Review Board: draws hours per member, planned against actual and the shares from the model items on its page, offline (0.7.0) |"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "b7ce35f238cd5ec1",
      "text": "# Linked Ledgers\nA double entry between two pages"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "511f4abddf458ec6",
      "text": "An entry that ends `for` or `to` a ledger records hours **I gave** to it. One that ends `from` or `by` a ledger records hours **I received**. Name a ledger on this same site with a wikilink, `for [[Alice Ledger]]`. Name a ledger on **another site** with an external link to the exact page, `for [https://alice.wiki/view/alices-ledger Alice's Ledger]` \u2014 it opens in a new tab and carries the site in its address. `http://`, `//site/slug`, ports, a trailing slash and a `.html` or `.json` suffix are accepted; write `/view/slug`, because a wiki answers that address and not a bare `/slug`. The other ledger confirms an entry with the opposite word and a link back to this page. A link without a direction word \u2014 `Meeting with [[David]]: 2 hours` \u2014 is an ordinary entry that happens to contain a link. The old `LEDGER:` command is gone since 0.3.0."
    },
    {
      "type": "timebank",
      "id": "99cdc1709ebb3b58",
      "text": "START: 1 September 2026\nEND: 7 September 2026\nNOTIFY: ntfy.sh/timebank-demo-david\nGardening for [[Alice Ledger]]: 2 hours\nRepairs from [[Alice Ledger]]: 1 hour\nA week of swaps with [[Alice Ledger]]."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "46a90ffb474d085b",
      "text": "A ledger is identified by its **site plus its slug**, never its title alone: two pages called David's Ledger on two sites are two ledgers and never match each other. A wikilink on the other ledger names the other ledger's own site, so it only points back here when both ledgers share a site; across sites the other ledger must name this one by external link. Two entries match when the other entry points back at this exact ledger, runs the opposite way, has the same minutes, and the same label once case, spacing and trailing punctuation are ignored. Each counter-entry confirms one entry only. When both items declare START/END the periods must overlap. On this about page Alice Ledger does not exist, so the badge above is red."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "2565e0c18332453d",
      "text": "# Verification Badge\nGreen, orange or red beside the column header"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "c882bdda56bd72d0",
      "text": "A ledger with linked entries shows a badge: **Verified** (green) when every linked entry is matched, **Partly verified** (orange) when some are, **Not verified** (red) when none are or a counterparty ledger cannot be reached. Hover for the detail: how many matched, which entries did not, and the address of the ledger that was checked. Each counterparty site named by external link is registered as a neighbour, so its flag shows and its pages resolve. The check runs in the browser; the last result is kept on the item as `item.verified`, with the time and the site that checked it."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "39023f1c68d65c2a",
      "text": "# Ledger Verification Tool\nClick the badge for a page that says what was checked"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "d7a03d9baa6c3c14",
      "text": "A single click on the badge opens the **Ledger Verification Tool** beside the ledger. It is a plugin page, shipped in this package's `pages/` and served on every site of the farm (green, like other plugin pages; also in the \u2630 menu). Its timebank item runs in `TOOL` mode: it finds the ledger on the page to its left, checks it and draws the report \u2014 which ledger, which site, when and from where it was checked, and the status. The **Entries** table puts the Result first, coloured like the badge: matched (green, *by page* when both ledgers link one transaction page), unmatched or ledger unreachable (red), with direction, hours, label, counterparty ledger and transaction page. **Transactions** gives each transaction page's sign-off: accepted, in dialogue or awaiting sign-off. **Freeze** writes pulled entries into the ledger. **Send verification message** posts one message per counterparty to the `NOTIFY:` [https://ntfy.sh ntfy] topic \u2014 title, status tags, a click link to the counterparty ledger, and a body of `Confirmed: \u2026 \u2014 matched` or `Verify: \u2026 \u2014 unmatched in <ledger address>` lines. **Find missing ledgers** lists candidates for every unreachable or unmatched counterparty, each with a **Fix** button that rewrites those entry lines to name it by external link, and lists entries with no transaction page as new pages to create from the Time Transaction Template. Freeze and Fix save through the wiki's page handler, so they need the browser to be logged in as the site owner, and say so when it is not. Double-click anywhere else on the ledger still opens the editor."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "e6446c2dc9c09da4",
      "text": "# Time Transaction Pages\nThe work behind the line"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "d2fe3e2f9d9226de",
      "text": "A ledger line is a summary; a **Time Transaction** page records the work \u2014 who gave, who received, how long, when, what was done \u2014 with a `transaction` item holding the same facts as data. The page is the record and the ledger a view of it; two ledgers whose lines agree still stand with no page behind them. Usually the giver writes the page on their own site from the site's Time Transaction Template, and the receiver signs off by adding the line to their ledger, forking the page first to comment or query."
    },
    {
      "type": "transaction",
      "id": "e94862f0a863aa8d",
      "text": "GIVER: [https://alice.wiki/view/alices-ledger Alice's Ledger]\nRECEIVER: [https://david.wiki/view/davids-ledger David's Ledger]\nHOURS: 1 hour\nDATE: 3 September 2026\nWHAT: Repairs\nSOURCE: hand\nPlaned the shed door and re-hung the gate."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "1b217a22efd4cfc9",
      "text": "The `transaction` item above (wiki-plugin-transaction, whose code ships in this package) draws the facts as a card and exposes them to the lineup the way map and calendar items do: the class `transaction-source` and a `transactionData()` function on its element. Name both ledgers by external link, so the page means the same on every site it is forked to."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "2bc43234fd5ad2f6",
      "text": "An entry's label may be a link to its transaction page \u2014 `[[Repairs for David, 3 September]] for [https://david.wiki/view/davids-ledger David's Ledger]: 1 hour` on the giver's site, `[https://alice.wiki/view/repairs-for-david-3-september Repairs for David, 3 September] from [https://alice.wiki/view/alices-ledger Alice's Ledger]: 1 hour` on the receiver's. When both ledgers link the same page, by site and slug, they match by page before any comparison of label and hours; label-and-hours matching stays the fallback."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "95b9ffe84f3c0f6b",
      "text": "A ledger with a `LINEUP` line **thaws**: it gathers transaction items from pages standing to its left in the lineup, and from the pages on its own site that link [[Time Transaction]] (as every page made from the template does), and shows the entries they imply for this ledger, marked *pulled*. A page already linked by a written line is never shown twice. **Freeze** \u2014 on the Ledger Verification Tool, or by saving the editor, which opens on the frozen text while entries are pulled \u2014 writes them into the ledger's own text, so it stands alone; `LINEUP` stays, so it keeps thawing."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "42faa381a28e2cdf",
      "text": "# From a Message\nAn audio note or a chat message becomes a page"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "0bc65afe61c982da",
      "text": "A transaction page is often made from a message. **Audio note**: record it with the Voice Tool (wiki-plugin-audionote), transcribe it, and fill the template \u2014 the speaker's ledger as GIVER, the ledger they name as RECEIVER, the time they give as HOURS, the transcript as the note, `SOURCE: audio note`, and the recording on the page. **Chat**: a Record message sent the [[Time Bank by Message]] way carries the same facts \u2014 who, for whom, how long, what \u2014 and fills the template the same way, with `SOURCE: chat message`. Either way the receiver then signs off in their own ledger, by hand or by Freeze."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "905c6397dc4b7e89",
      "text": "# Thank You Invoice\n> The receiver records; the giver reconciles."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "1f13fbb1a2d6ade7",
      "text": "A thank-you is written by the **receiver**: a Time Transaction page on the receiver's own site and the line in their ledger \u2014 Marvin, the Pi5's Telegram bot, does it from a `/thanks` message or a voice note after reading it back in the chat ([https://time.peoplepowered.money/view/thank-you-invoice Thank You Invoice]). Nothing is pushed to the giver. The giver's ledger **watches** the sites on its `WATCH:` lines and the sites of the ledgers it already names; every transaction page there that names it and that it does not carry yet is **awaiting reconcile** \u2014 the badge reads *Verified \u00b7 6h awaiting reconcile*, and the rows show under the ledger without counting in its total."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "2efaff09394e82fb",
      "text": "On the [[Ledger Verification Tool]], **Awaiting reconcile** lists those pages and offers **Reconcile**: it opens each one beside the tool, forks it to the ledger's site with the wiki's own fork, writes the lines into the ledger \u2014 keeping the link to the page where it was written, so the pair matches by page \u2014 and checks again. It needs the browser logged in as the site owner, and says so when it is not. An Energy Invoice is the same page from the giver's side, awaiting reconcile on the receiver's ledger."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "c024403f477a3095",
      "text": "# Periods and the Summary\n> One ledger page per month, and a summary that adds them up."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "3d42a399e0b9d7ee",
      "text": "Years of transactions do not belong in one item. Keep a ledger page per month \u2014 **Alice's Ledger 2026-09**, with its own START, END, entries and badge \u2014 and a summary ledger, **Alice's Ledger**, whose item holds `OWNER: [[About Alice]]` and `PERIODS: 10`. PERIODS finds the period pages by title prefix in the site's sitemap and shows each period's badge and hours, the ten most recent transactions and the **net balance** \u2014 hours given less hours received on the lines written in the period ledgers. Its badge is the worst of the periods' badges: any fail is a fail, any partial a partial."
    },
    {
      "type": "timebank",
      "id": "18e308735f67d44d",
      "text": "OWNER: [[About Alice]]\nPERIODS: 10\nNOTIFY: ntfy.sh/timebank-demo-david"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "1528e0b3bce00352",
      "text": "The item above has no period pages on this site, so it says how to start one. The summary's title is the ledger's identity: transaction pages and other ledgers name Alice's Ledger, never a period page, and a period page matches as the ledger it belongs to. A counterparty that is a summary is read through its period pages overlapping the period being checked. A period ledger with LINEUP pulls only the transaction pages dated inside it."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "12eec31662d5240d",
      "text": "# Names and Occasions\n> The work and the person, never the date."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "43f0f527dd0970ce",
      "text": "A transaction page is named for the work and the person \u2014 Childcare for David \u2014 and its date lives in the transaction item's DATE line and a date item beside it. When the same work recurs for the same person the page recurs: one transaction item per occasion, each with its own date, and an occasion's identity is page plus item. A ledger line names an occasion by linking the page with the day first \u2014 `2026-09-10 [[Childcare for David]] for [http://david.localhost:4242/view/davids-ledger David's Ledger]: 4 hours`. Two lines pair by page, then by occasion: the same day when both are dated, else the same minutes; lines dated on different days never pair."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "8bdd652a54f3db36",
      "text": "# Index and Balance\n> Every transaction page on a site, and the balance beside its owner."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "015f7bb8b32fc721",
      "text": "The [[Transactions Index]] is a plugin page like the [[Ledger Verification Tool]]: its item holds INDEX, and for the site of the page to its left it lists every page linking Time Transaction \u2014 less the template, the topic and ledgers \u2014 one row per occasion with date, parties, hours and state: **logged** (a period ledger has its line), **awaiting** (the period ledger holding its date pulls it) or **orphan**. A party whose ledger cannot be read is flagged unknown. It opens the pages as a lineup, and Log the orphans writes their lines in for the site's owner."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "d3881620c73e8f5f",
      "text": "On the owner's About page an item holding `BALANCE: [[Alice's Ledger]]` shows hours given, received and the net, read live from the period ledgers, with links to the ledger and the index. See the test bed: [http://alice.localhost:4242/view/about-alice/view/transactions-index About Alice beside the Transactions Index]."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "fb17c6710a3875d0",
      "text": "# Review and Board\n> The week approved once, and the picture for the room."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "85200a846f4c71fc",
      "text": "On a weekly report a timebank item holding `REVIEW: 2026-W40` reads the model item above it that freezes the week's rows of the Review Model's Review tab \u2014 planned, actual, variance \u2014 and offers **Approve the week**. Only the site owner can approve; logged out, it says so and saves nothing. Approving writes `APPROVED: 2026-10-05 by David Bovill` and each member's hours into the item, once; the broker's report tool burns them into the workbook's Approved sheet and refreezes. An item holding `BOARD` draws the [[Review Board]] from the model items on its own page: hours per member, planned against actual per week, and the shares \u2014 plain SVG, so the room needs no network."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "b7d3f19e42a80c11",
      "text": "# Repositories\n\nSource on GitHub: https://github.com/Hitchhikers-Guide-to-the-Galaxy/wiki-plugin-timebank\n\nPackage on npm: https://www.npmjs.com/package/wiki-plugin-timebank"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "a1b2c3d4e5001001",
      "text": "# Version"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "a1b2c3d4e5001002",
      "text": "**Version:** 0.7.0\n\n**Built:** Fri, 25 Sep 2026\n\n**Change:** The Review Model. REVIEW mode on a weekly report \u2014 the week's planned against actual, frozen from the Review Model by the Model Plugin, and an owner-only Approve that burns the week once; BOARD mode draws the Review Board from the frozen figures on its page, offline, with a Full screen button. The broker's tool gains --review-model, --plan and --approve and writes the workbook with cached values."
    }
  ],
